+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Attenuation Leakage:** If view and light directions are perpendicular, a 2D shadow buffer approach causes blurry shadows and dark surfaces due to coarse resolution interpolation [S8, book]. Half-angle slicing mitigates this by decoupling light transport resolution from volume resolution, but artifacts may still appear at shadow boundaries where opacity jumps sharply [S8, book].
+*   **Push Constants Size:** The Eye Pass push constants total 144 bytes, exceeding the Vulkan minimum guarantee of 128 bytes. Requires drivers supporting $\ge 256$ bytes [S4, S7, unofficial].
+*   **Single Light Source:** Supports only one directional or point light source located outside the volume [S1].
+*   **Slice Spacing:** To maintain consistent visual density, the slice spacing along the half-vector ($d_s$) must be adjusted based on the angle $\theta$ between view and light directions ($d_v$):
+    $$ d_s = \frac{d_v}{\cos(\theta/2)} $$
+    [S8, book]
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Two-pass volume rendering
-Uses two passes per slice but typically aligns slices with the view or light direction, requiring separate geometries or more complex state management for shadows [S2, unofficial]. Half-angle slicing uses a single slice geometry for both passes [S2, unofficial][S5, unofficial].
-
-### Two-Pass Ray Marching Volume Rendering
-Uses ray marching within passes rather than slice-based rasterization [S6, book]. Half-angle slicing relies on hardware rasterization of proxy geometry, which is generally faster for large volumes but less accurate for complex internal structures [S2, unofficial].
-
-### Multi-pass GPU Ray-Casting with Early Ray Termination
-Uses ray casting with early termination to save computation [S3]. Half-angle slicing does not inherently support early termination in the same way, as it relies on fixed slice traversal, but it avoids the overhead of per-ray intersection tests [S3].
+### [Multiple-Scattering Angular Distribution Estimation](15670-multiple-scattering-angular-distribution-estimation.md)
+Extends half-angle slicing to approximate the angular distribution of light after multiple scattering events by convolving the particle phase function with itself, handling gradual angular spreading rather than just unscattered or diffuse light.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of slices and $S$ is the number of samples per slice (determined by rasterization coverage) [G]. The cost is dominated by two rasterization passes per slice [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \cdot H)$ for the light and eye buffers, where $W, H$ are buffer dimensions. This is significantly lower than 3D shadow maps $O(V^3)$ [S6, book].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves high-resolution shadow effects without pre-computation [S2, unofficial]. Performance is limited by the number of slices and the resolution of the light buffer [S2, unofficial]. Using "render to texture" extensions avoids costly copies between buffers [S6, book].
-*   **Strengths:** Decouples light transport from volume resolution; avoids 3D shadow map storage; minimizes slice count compared to view-aligned slicing for oblique angles [S4, unofficial][S6, book].
-*   **Limitations:** Artifacts at shadow boundaries if opacity jumps sharply (mitigated by higher light buffer resolution) [S6, book]. Attenuation leakage (blurry shadows) can occur if view and light are perpendicular and a 2D buffer is used without sufficient resolution [S6, book]. Requires GPU driver support for large push constants (256 bytes) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When volumetric shadows are required and the light/view angle is not perpendicular [S6, book]. Not suitable for real-time if the number of slices must be very high to resolve fine volume details, as it doubles the rasterization cost per slice [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot R)$, where $N$ is the number of slices and $R$ is the resolution of the slice proxy geometry (typically constant per frame). Two passes per slice.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \cdot H)$ for the Light and Eye buffers, where $W, H$ are buffer dimensions. Significantly less than 3D shadow maps [S8, book].
+*   **Performance:** Enables interactive frame rates for volumetric shadows [S1, S2]. Performance is limited by the cost of generating proxy geometry and two rasterization passes per slice.
+*   **Strengths:** Produces hard, well-defined shadows; supports indirect lighting and multiple scattering; avoids pre-computation of shadow maps; efficient memory usage compared to 3D shadow volumes/maps [S1, S5][S8, book].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited to single light sources; artifacts at shadow boundaries with sharp opacity transitions; requires high-resolution light buffers to minimize leakage; push constant size constraints on some GPUs [S1][S4, unofficial][S8, book].
+*   **When to use:** Interactive applications requiring dynamic volumetric shadows and translucent material rendering [S1, S2].
+*   **When not to use:** Scenes with multiple dynamic light sources or complex global illumination requiring full 3D light transport storage [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of GPU-based volumetric shadow rendering, specifically proposed to avoid the storage and pre-computation costs of volumetric shadow maps [S2, unofficial][S6, book]. It was detailed in *GPU Gems* (Chapter 39) and the *Visualization Handbook* [S1][S6, book]. The technique leverages the geometric property that the half-angle vector provides a common slicing axis for both eye and light visibility [S2, unofficial].
+Half-angle slicing was introduced by Kniss et al. in 2002 [S1, S2][S8, book]. It was the first slice-based approach to synchronize rendering with illumination computation, integrating volumetric illumination models including indirect lighting and scattering within interactive applications [S1]. It builds on the concept of the half-angle vector for volume shadow computation [S5]. The technique is described in detail in "Interactive Translucent Volume Rendering and Procedural Modeling" [S5] and "Multi-Dimensional Transfer Functions for Volume Rendering" [S8, book]. It is often cited as Kniss et al. [51] or [14] in literature [S2][S3, unofficial].
 
